Use one of these, depending on what you need:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    return 0;          // Clean success exit
}
```

Or from anywhere in the program:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);    // Clean success exit
exit(EXIT_FAILURE);    // Clean failure exit
```

Key difference:

- `return` from `main` ends the program cleanly.
- `exit(...)` terminates the whole process and runs registered `atexit()` handlers, flushes standard I/O buffers.
- `_Exit(...)` or `quick_exit(...)` are more abrupt options when you specifically do not want normal cleanup.

Example error termination:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    FILE *f = fopen("data.txt", "r");
    if (!f) {
        perror("fopen");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    fclose(f);
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```
